{
  "_comment": "Monster catalog (real data drives the schema). Each monster carries combat stats, visual identity (tile, color), movement rules, and arrays for `spells` (castable abilities), `on_hit_effects` (effects applied on a successful hit), and `passives` (always-on traits). v2 transforms: monster `passives[].type` and `on_hit_effects[].type` are renamed to `effect_id`, referencing entries in effects.json (drain, poisoned, slowed, consumed, regen, fire_resistance, poison_immunity). Per-monster overrides (chance, amount, duration, etc.) stay as flat keys alongside `effect_id`. Monster `spells[]` remain inline for now — many monster spell types don't have player-castable Spell records, so unifying with spells.json is deferred. Dropped per the not-used rule: monster `description` (parsed but never read), `spawn_weight` (TS uses encounters.json weights), the `ranged` sub-object (entire object parsed but unread), and the top-level `spawn_tables` (TS uses encounters.json).",
